WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.583
Where exactly does a cascade get its gain?

2
00:00:06.523 --> 00:00:08.643
Follow one photon down the rod's ladder.

3
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:16.267
Step one. Activated rhodopsin switches on transducin, which switches on PDE6.

4
00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:21.227
Yue and colleagues counted each active unit as one effective complex.

5
00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:28.620
Step two. Each effective complex breaks down cyclic GMP, many molecules each.

6
00:00:29.633 --> 00:00:35.713
Step three. The fall in cyclic GMP closes cyclic-nucleotide-gated channels.

7
00:00:36.783 --> 00:00:42.963
In mouse rods, about 2,000 cyclic GMP molecules are hydrolysed for each absorbed

8
00:00:42.963 --> 00:00:43.963
photon.

9
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:50.427
In a separate study, Yue and colleagues estimated about 12 to 14 effective complexes

10
00:00:50.427 --> 00:00:52.307
per activated rhodopsin.

11
00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:59.007
Predict. If these two estimates can be combined, which step multiplies the signal

12
00:00:59.007 --> 00:01:00.007
more?

13
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.020
If they combine, step two.

14
00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.300
Each complex would break down roughly 150 molecules.

15
00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.620
That is an illustration, not a measured yield.

16
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.800
But other analyses put step one several times higher.

17
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.860
On those, the two factors could be comparable.

18
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.930
Either way, both steps amplify.

19
00:01:25.870 --> 00:01:30.750
The gain belongs to particular enzymes, not to the receptor being a G protein

20
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:31.750
coupled receptor.

21
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.270
A receptor step need not amplify at all.

22
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:41.350
In frog olfactory neurons, one bound receptor rarely activates even one G protein.

23
00:01:42.483 --> 00:01:44.943
There, the gain is thought to come at later steps.

24
00:01:45.963 --> 00:01:49.303
Adenylyl cyclase makes cyclic AMP, which opens channels.

25
00:01:50.203 --> 00:01:52.543
The calcium that enters opens a chloride current.

26
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:59.407
Now the price. Every rung is an encounter between molecules, or an enzyme turning

27
00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:03.447
over. So the current rises only after several steps have run.

28
00:02:04.417 --> 00:02:09.517
In mouse olfactory neurons, the earliest first spikes came about 25 to 40

29
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:11.997
milliseconds after a strong odour pulse.

30
00:02:13.017 --> 00:02:16.857
That is a latency to a spike, which includes more than transduction.

31
00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:19.357
It is not a channel time constant.

32
00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:24.067
Spontaneous receptor activity can also enter the cascade.

33
00:02:25.187 --> 00:02:29.287
Neurons with different odorant receptors showed different levels of spontaneous

34
00:02:29.287 --> 00:02:30.287
activity.

35
00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.740
Inverse agonist odorants could quiet it.

36
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:39.040
A cascade that multiplies a receptor event multiplies a spontaneous one too.

37
00:02:40.033 --> 00:02:43.173
So enzymes between stimulus and pore can buy gain.

38
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:48.073
They often pay in time, and they can amplify spontaneous activity too.
